\date{2026-06-09}
\title{Conjunctive types}
\author{fredrikbakke}
\taxon{Module}
\import{stt-macros}

\transclude{stt-00AB}

\agda{
open import Foundations.Universes
open import Algebra.Lattice

module Synthetic.Conjunctive {𝓘} (Δ¹ : Type 𝓘) (I : Lattice Δ¹) where

open Lattice I renaming (0l to i0; 1l to i1)

open import Algebra.Lattice.Instances.Opposite

import Synthetic.Disjunctive
module Conj = Synthetic.Disjunctive.Disj Δ¹ (Lattice-op I)
}

\subtree[stt-00TW]{
\title{The conjunctive property}
\author{fredrikbakke}
\date{2026-06-15}

\p{
The generic interval does not necessarily satisfy \em{the conjunctive property}, in
the sense that for all #{i} and #{j} such that #{(i ∧ j) = 0} we would have #{i = 0}
or #{j = 0}. Instead, we can speak of particular types which \em{'see'} the
interval as satisfying this property. This can be made precise by asking for types
which are [null](stt-005G) with respect to the family of propositions
#{(i = 0) ∨ (j = 0)} for all #{i} and #{j} such that #{(i ∧ j) = 0}.
}

\p{
The concept of conjunctive types is the formal dual to disjunctive types, and we
formalise this concept as such, by reexporting the lemmas from
\ref{Synthetic.Disjunctive} under new names, instantiated at the opposite lattice.
}
}

\agda{
open import Synthetic.Disjunctive Δ¹ (Lattice-op I) public
  using ()
  renaming
    ( ∨² to ∧²
    ; fam-disjunctive to fam-conjunctive
